Ideally we'd cancel the original future when the wrapper is cancelled to get the same behavior as running without the wrapper. We could use
```java
    CompletableFuture<T> result = new CompletableFuture<T>() {
      @Override
      public boolean cancel(boolean mayInterruptIfRunning) {
        boolean cancelled = super.cancel(mayInterruptIfRunning);
        if (cancelled) {
          future.cancel(mayInterruptIfRunning);
        }
        return cancelled;
      }
    };
```
but this won't work when a stage is added to the future and that stage is cancelled instead. To handle that we'd need to override `newIncompleteFuture` like in https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java-instrumentation/blob/main/instrumentation/java-http-client/library/src/main/java/io/opentelemetry/instrumentation/javahttpclient/internal/CompletableFutureWrapper.java but that method was added in java9. I think not handling it isn't a problem when underlying future doesn't handle it either which seems to be the case here. In the test the aws sdk seems to chain a stage on top of the wrapper so handling the cancellation in our wrapper won't really help. The underlying future cancels the request when it is completed exceptionally so this PR should work nicely.

